title Three laws, one roof -- the first Glow core lands with arm-parity to its standing desks
prompt Keaton approved the crux ruling and said kg; the core rune lap was granted.
think The alphabet had already reserved the spellings -- the pipe-percent, plus-plus, and dash-dash digraphs stood in the 30-pair table -- so the form cost no census cascade. Two new modules carry it: glow/rune_shop_core.rye parses the core line-level (open, up to eight plus-plus arms each handing its two gate lines whole to rune_shop_gate.parse_source, close), so composition arrives with zero new gate grammar; glow/lower_shop_core.rye emits one function per arm and a speaking argv main dispatching by arm ordinal.
think glow_run needed three walls taught: the six-line ceiling steps aside for the form alone (the core names its own, two plus three per arm), the argv line-count wall likewise, and the core routes ahead of the six-line collect its arm count would overflow. The worker gained the core stem's two-or-three-sample lane.
think The desk gate-lantern-face-core.glow holds the face's three proven laws as arms -- the 38-byte wall, the unknown-carrying-an-answer refusal, the feed's kind law. The witness holds every arm to its standing single desk's answers on the same samples by whole-output equality through the same worker, and proves three refusals: an unnamed arm ordinal, an unclosed core (MalformedCore), a core with no arms (EmptyCore), the last two from tracked fixtures.
think The tree's guards caught two of my faults mid-lap again: copyForwards molted to tally's copy_disjoint before the first compile, and a compound assert split on the TAME ban. The opening triads seated on both new files.
obs This is the door the crux ruling named: the language now authors a module, not only a law. The named next steps queue behind it honestly -- cords so arms can speak text, gate-calls-gate, and the far oracle of a lowered module passing the hand-written face witness unchanged.
obs Committed locally under the push hold -- Mind read five seconds fresh at the close, still mid-lap toward its sign.
